known for her signature white headscarf  de bonafini became the symbol of a  mother's struggle after The  Disappearance of her two sons  I always say that Lions grew inside me  when they took my children wearing the  scarf makes me feel taller more composed  it feels like the Embrace of my children  in the late 1970s she founded the  mothers of the plaza de Mayo along with  other women to demand the return of  their children taken by Security Forces  they're worn nappies on their heads  later swapping them for white scarves an  emblem of their movement  de bonafini worked tirelessly to expose  the fate of the estimated 30 000  argentinians who disappeared during the  so-called dirty war in the 70s and 80s  although her own Sons were never found  she continued her campaign even after  the dictatorship ended becoming one of  Argentina's most passionate and  outspoken rights activists  even today more than 40 years after  their first March the mothers of the  plaza de Mayo remains a powerful force  holding Gatherings to continue de  bonifini's Legacy  she taught us not to lower our heads and  not to cry in front of the enemy to be  seen as strong even if the tears came we  had to go out into the street with a  heads held high proud of the children we  had our kidnapped children  on hearing the news of her death  President Alberto Fernandez declared  three days of national mourning calling  her a tireless fighter de bonafini may  have divided Argentina in her later  years with her controversial comments  and political affiliations but the pain  of her early days unified a nation and  turned her into a figure of struggle  driven by a need for answers and justice
 

